ork.\012   Firstmate'\''s reply normally writes that closing line at answer time; when a blocker or wait clears WITHOUT a firstmate reply, append `resolved [at=<epoch>]: {how it cleared}` yourself (same `[key=<slug>]` if you opened it with one) as you resume.\0127. Never stop, restart, or update the shared `no-mistakes` daemon - it is one instance serving\012   every lane/home, so restarting it kills other lanes'\'' in-flight pipeline runs; only firstmate\012   manages the daemon.\012   Before you append `blocked:` about the pipeline, run `no-mistakes daemon status` and\012   `no-mistakes axi status`. If the daemon socket refuses connections or is missing, append\012   `blocked [at=<epoch>]: {the daemon error}` and stop even when the local run record still says running or\012   fixing, because that record can be stale after the daemon exits. A run record failed with a\012   daemon error is also a real block.\012   Only after ruling out socket refusal, if the run is still running or fixing, reattach and keep\012   going. A drive-call error, timeout, slow read, or generic unreachability is NOT a daemon error:\012   the daemon accepts `respond` immediately and runs the round in the background, so a killed or\012   timed-out call was only waiting for a read while the run kept working.\012\012# Firstmate instruction inbox\012Firstmate steers you through durable message files in '\''/Users/irene/Developer/kunchenguid_repos/firstmate/state/imac-claude-agents-md-migration-20260924.inbox'\''.\012When a terminal message says an instruction is waiting there - and at any natural checkpoint when you are unsure - list '\''/Users/irene/Developer/kunchenguid_repos/firstmate/state/imac-claude-agents-md-migration-20260924.inbox'\''/*.msg, read and act on each message in numeric order, then acknowledge each handled message by moving it: `mv '\''/Users/irene/Developer/kunchenguid_repos/firstmate/state/imac-claude-agents-md-migration-20260924.inbox'\''/NNN.msg '\''/Users/irene/Developer